From 4a6c27b437c90a068b0b944eaa9b1910e6d237c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:56:27 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 16/32] Assemble: look at every slotless device for the newest
 superblock

Assemble() bases the array on the superblock of the newest device and
looks for it only at the even entries of best[]: the odd ones are the
replacements of the active slots.  Devices without a slot (raid_disk -1)
do not come in pairs, though: they get consecutive entries from
raid_disks * 2 on.  When the members of a container report no slot, as
AMD RAIDXpert2 disks do, and the newest one sits at an odd entry while
the even ones hold older disks, no device is chosen and the container
does not assemble: "No suitable drives found".

That is the case after a degraded boot: the disk that was absent comes
back with an older configuration, and with "mdadm --assemble --scan" it
is found first when it comes first in /proc/partitions (a Debian
initramfs: the root array does not assemble, the boot hangs in
local-block).  With the disks named in the other order it assembles.

Look at the even entries first, as before, then at the odd ones.  The
choice changes only where none was found before: when no active slot
holds an up-to-date device with the newest superblock.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
---
 Assemble.c | 17 +++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Assemble.c b/Assemble.c
index cff24a59..08a3f92b 100644
--- a/Assemble.c
+++ b/Assemble.c
@@ -1380,7 +1380,7 @@ int Assemble(char *mddev, struct mddev_ident *ident,
 	int devcnt;
 	unsigned int okcnt, sparecnt, rebuilding_cnt, journalcnt;
 	int journal_clean = 0;
-	int i;
+	int i, n, half;
 	int was_forced = 0;
 	int most_recent = 0;
 	int chosen_drive;
@@ -1713,9 +1713,18 @@ try_again:
 	 */
 	chosen_drive = -1;
 	st->ss->free_super(st);
-	for (i=0; chosen_drive < 0 && i<bestcnt; i+=2) {
-		int j = best[i];
-		int fd;
+	/*
+	 * The active slots (even entries) first, then the odd ones: devices
+	 * without a slot - the members of a container whose metadata gives
+	 * none - get consecutive entries, so the newest of them may be at an
+	 * odd one.
+	 */
+	half = (bestcnt + 1) / 2;
+	for (n = 0; chosen_drive < 0 && n < bestcnt; n++) {
+		int j, fd;
+
+		i = n < half ? n * 2 : (n - half) * 2 + 1;
+		j = best[i];
 
 		if (j<0)
 			continue;
